{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgzBojMgxPD1C1x1h9YVq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"It isn't a leftover, but the tests don't need it.\n\n**What it does:** `kiln/timeparse.py:3-13` defines it. With `KILN_LEGACY_TZ=1`, `parse()` reads naive timestamps (no offset) as UTC, which is how kiln behaved before 2.0. Without it, naive timestamps are read as local time. It has been in SETUP.md since the initial commit, `d274cef`.\n\n**Does step 4 need it?** I don't think so. The only test touching naive timestamps is `test_naive_parses` in `tests/test_timeparse.py`. It checks `.hour == 8`, and that holds whether the timestamp is read as UTC or as local time. This matches my earlier run, where the tests passed both with and without the variable.\n\n**What I couldn't tell:** I don't know why the variable was put in SETUP.md. Possibly it's a workaround from before the tests were written that way, or it's meant to match what the seed data or CI expect. The git history only has the one commit, so it gives no reason.\n\nI haven't changed anything. If you want it removed from step 4, I can make that a separate commit. Before that, I'd check whether `seed.py`, `doctor.py` or CI relies on the legacy behavior."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":295,"cache_read_input_tokens":22177,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":295},"output_tokens":410,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"<uuid-1>","uuid":"<uuid-57>","timestamp":"2026-11-13T03:05:30.670Z","request_id":"req_011CgzBoj93qRQHyDcKunACF"}
